{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook and the turbulent personal and legal battles that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is a gripping blend of drama, tension, and sharp dialogue that feels as fast-paced as the tech boom it portrays. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing his brilliance, social awkwardness, and emotional detachment with uncanny precision. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a man driven by ambition and wounded by rejection.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally compelling. andrew garfield shines as justin timberlake’s character, erik, whose loyalty and betrayal form the emotional core of the story, while jesse plemons and jordan belfort (as sean parker) bring depth and charisma to their roles. parker, in particular, is portrayed as a chaotic, visionary force who both inspires and destabilizes zuckerberg’s journey.\n\nsorkin’s screenplay is a triumph—witty, dense, and rhythmically brilliant. the dialogue crackles with intelligence, and the courtroom scenes, framed as flashbacks, add structure and suspense, turning legal testimony into high-stakes drama. the pacing never falters, and every scene serves a purpose, whether it’s advancing the plot or revealing character.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is meticulous. the film’s cold, blue-toned aesthetic mirrors the digital world it depicts, and the use of lighting and framing creates a sense of isolation even in crowded rooms. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is haunting and minimalist, perfectly complementing the film’s tone with electronic pulses that echo the heartbeat of the internet age.\n\nbeyond its technical achievements, the film raises profound questions about ownership, friendship, and the cost of innovation. it doesn’t glorify zuckerberg but presents him as a complex figure—neither villain nor hero—whose actions changed the world while leaving personal relationships in ruins. the ending, with zuckerberg refreshing his ex-girlfriend’s facebook page, is a quiet, devastating moment that lingers long after the credits roll.\n\nthe social network is more than a biopic; it’s a modern tragedy about connection in the age of disconnection. it’s smart, stylish, and emotionally resonant, and it remains one of the most important films of the 2010s.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -0.9510456919670105}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.007622679229825735}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.67808997631073}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.458328112377785e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0003233625029679388}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.7643094658851624}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -8.702239938429557e-06}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -1.0632712841033936}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.6117238998413086}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.0636947751045227}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -0.07916721701622009}, {"token": " battles", "logprob": -0.0401872955262661}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.2037440538406372}, {"token": " surrounded", "logprob": -0.5981115102767944}, {"token": " its",